// ## 18. TYPECASTING

// **Definition:** Typecasting is the process of converting one data type into another data type.

// **Example:**

// ```cpp
// double x = 45.89;

// int y = (int)x;

// cout << y;
// ```

// **Output:**

// ```text
// 45
// ```

// **Explanation:**

// The decimal part is removed when a floating-point value is converted to an integer using this cast.

// ### Types of Typecasting

// ### 1. Implicit Typecasting

// **Definition:** Conversion performed automatically by the compiler.

// **Example:**

// ```cpp
// int a = 10;
// double b = a;

// cout << b;
// ```

// **Output:**

// ```text
// 10
// ```

// ### 2. Explicit Typecasting

// **Definition:** Conversion performed manually by the programmer.

// **Example:**

// ```cpp
// double a = 10.75;

// int b = (int)a;

// cout << b;
// ```

// **Output:**

// ```text
// 10
// ```

// ### Difference Between Implicit and Explicit Typecasting

// | Implicit Typecasting | Explicit Typecasting |
// |---|---|
// | Automatic conversion | Manual conversion |
// | Performed by compiler | Performed by programmer |
// | No cast is written | Cast is written |
// | Example: `double b = a;` | Example: `int b = (int)a;` |

