// ## 20. INCREMENT AND DECREMENT OPERATORS

// **Definition:** Increment and decrement operators are used to increase or decrease the value of a variable by 1.

// ### A. Increment Operator (++)

// The increment operator increases the value of a variable by 1.

// #### 1. Pre-Increment (++x)

// **Definition:** First increases the value by 1 and then uses the updated value.

// **Example:**

// ```cpp
// int x = 10;

// cout << ++x;
// ```

// **Output:**

// ```text
// 11
// ```

// #### 2. Post-Increment (x++)

// **Definition:** First uses the current value and then increases it by 1.

// **Example:**

// ```cpp
// int x = 10;

// cout << x++;
// ```

// **Output:**

// ```text
// 10
// ```

// ### B. Decrement Operator (--)

// The decrement operator decreases the value of a variable by 1.

// #### 1. Pre-Decrement (--x)

// **Definition:** First decreases the value by 1 and then uses the updated value.

// **Example:**

// ```cpp
// int x = 10;

// cout << --x;
// ```

// **Output:**

// ```text
// 9
// ```

// #### 2. Post-Decrement (x--)

// **Definition:** First uses the current value and then decreases it by 1.

// **Example:**

// ```cpp
// int x = 10;

// cout << x--;
// ```

// **Output:**

// ```text
// 10
// ```

// ### Difference Between Pre and Post Operators

// | Operator | Meaning | Working |
// |---|---|---|
// | `++x` | Pre-Increment | Increase first, then use |
// | `x++` | Post-Increment | Use first, then increase |
// | `--x` | Pre-Decrement | Decrease first, then use |
// | `x--` | Post-Decrement | Use first, then decrease |

